proof_plan:
Completing the square:
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn | l = \map {\hat g} \xi
| r = \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-\pi \paren {x + i \xi}^2} e^{-\pi \xi^2} \rd x
}}
{{eqn | r = e^{-\pi \xi^2} \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-\pi x^2} \rd x
| c = [[Cauchy's Integral Theorem]]
}}
{{eqn | r = e^{-\pi \xi^2}
| c = [[Gaussian Integral]]
}}
{{end-eqn}}

From $\map {\paren {\FF^2 f} } x = \map f {-x}$, we have $\FF^4 = I$.
Hence the eigenvalues of $\FF$ lie in $\set {1, -i, -1, i}$, with the [[Definition:Hermite Function|Hermite functions]] as eigenfunctions and $g$ the first of them.
{{qed}}
